[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next fork — and it's the one the repo actually forces.

  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is loaded as a classic [38;5;153m<script>[39m with plain globals; [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are CommonJS and are never
  loaded by the page. There's no bundler and no build step. So "put the validator in a shared module" has to pick a
  module system, and that choice propagates to every future form and to how the code can be tested.

  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153msrc/validation.js[39m exports rules and a [38;5;153mvalidate()[39m; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches to [38;5;153m<script type="module" [39m
    [38;5;153msrc="app.js">[39m. Buys real [38;5;153mimport[39m syntax, no globals, works natively in browsers with no build step. Costs: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
    becomes a module (it must be served over http, not opened as [38;5;153mfile://[39m), and [38;5;153msrc/[39m ends up mixed ESM/CommonJS unless
    the old files move too. Hard to reverse cheaply once forms import it.
  - [1mGlobal namespace[0m — [38;5;153mvalidation.js[39m assigns [38;5;153mwindow.FormValidation[39m; forms read it via a second [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag. Buys zero
    disruption — matches how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m already works, still opens from [38;5;153mfile://[39m. Costs: globals, load-order coupling, and
    it's awkward to unit-test outside a browser.
  - [1mCommonJS + bundler[0m — matches [38;5;153msrc/[39m, but adds a toolchain to a repo with zero dependencies. I'd rule this out as
    disproportionate.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m It's the only option that gives the shared module a real interface and makes it
  testable outside a browser, and it needs no dependencies. The one genuine cost is the [38;5;153mfile://[39m limitation.
